module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "A module cites a STANDARD and models a decision procedure, but the decision the code implements is a weaker or different rule than the standard's normative one. The citation then attests properties the code does not have -- total order, precedence, canonicality -- and every reader who trusts the anchor inherits them. The anchor is current, the name resolves, and the sentence the code speaks is not the sentence the standard speaks.",

    "**THIS IS NOT THE TOOL-CLI/STANDARD MISMATCH, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE CLASS.** The neighbour class (fixed in gunbc#13189's review) is about WHO owns the surface: a module models a tool's CLI while citing the standard the tool implements. Here the owner is right -- the module really does model what the cited standard defines -- and what is wrong is the STRENGTH: the code path under the declaration implements a weaker rule than the standard's normative algorithm. That is worse on two counts. The pairing survives the placement gate, which correctly reads subject-to-authority ownership; and the citation is plausible on its face, so review has nothing visible to flag until someone reads the code path against the standard's text.",

    "**RECEIPT (vivid-ibex-684, 2026-10-04, gunbc#13189).** `dag/extdeps/version/semver.dag` scopes `semver_scheme` with a first citation to semver.org (Semantic Versioning 2.0.0), whose normative content for comparison is the PRECEDENCE algorithm in its section 11: identifiers composed only of digits compare numerically; identifiers containing letters or hyphens compare lexically in ASCII sort order; numeric identifiers ALWAYS have lower precedence than non-numeric ones (so `1.0.0-alpha` ranks below `1.0.0`); and a larger set of pre-release fields has higher precedence when all preceding identifiers are equal. The comparison path under the module calls `semver_identity_compare` -- a lexical string comparison that implements none of those rules and ranks `1.10.0` below `1.9.0`, the reverse of the standard's numeric order. Found by external side-chat review as REQUESTED CHANGES (`review 5404350654`), not by any mechanism in the tree; the module and its regenerated mirror were pulled back to the frontier byte-identical rather than fixed, so the defect remains live in the tree as of this row.",

    "**RECEIPT (vivid-ibex-684, 2026-10-04, gunbc#13258, side-chat review `5406002589`; defect live in the tree).** `dag/extdeps/accessibility/wcag_contrast.dag` cites WCAG 2.2 (www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#dfn-relative-luminance) and its comment asserts the linearization knee is the page's published constant at 0.03928 -- and the code does use 392/10000. But the cited definition specifies the threshold 0.04045 and notes 0.03928 as the older value, so the citation attests a definition the code does not implement. The module was authored 2026-07-21 with the comment noting the page as retrieved that day, so the pairing was already false when authored -- there is no upstream-drift story here, only a misattestation that name-level checks (live URL, subject ownership) do not see. Pulled from #13258 per that review rather than fixed; the defect is live as of this receipt.",

    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-05, frontier; semver half).** `dag/extdeps/version/semver.dag`'s `semver_identity_compare` now parses the label back into the typed form over the SemVer 2.0.0 section 2 grammar and routes through `semver_compare`, the module's one implementation of the section 11 precedence rule; a label outside the grammar yields none from the scheme's compare -- a typed, located refusal of the precedence question, pinned positively by `w_semver_scheme_out_of_grammar_label_refuses_precedence` -- and the interface (`extdeps.version` `VersionScheme.compare`) was widened to `Ordering?` so the refusal is a value, with the sibling git and go scheme instances updated. The cited source was re-read live first (semver.org/#spec-item-11, retrieved 2026-10-05). Five discriminating reds failed for the documented reason before the fix (`1.10.0` vs `1.9.0` ranked Less; release above pre-release reversed; numeric-vs-alphanumeric and numeric-width pre-release cases ranked by ASCII; build metadata ranked) and pass after (dag/test/claim/extdeps_version_semver_witness_test.dag, remote claim_batch --claim-run). The regenerated stage0 mirrors are installed and `claim_executor --required-regen` now adjudicates the whole tree clean (declared_divergent=1 [main.rs] only). The wcag half of this row is repaired in a separate PR (#13414).",

    "**WHY NO EXISTING WALL SEES IT, stated as a boundary rather than a gap to be closed by the neighbour.** The extdeps scope placement gate adjudicates the diff-added pairing of a subject declaration to a citation anchor at the level of NAMES: it can see that a subject is not the cited upstream's surface (tool CLI vs standard), because that mismatch is visible in what the module declares and what the anchor names. The lexical-vs-precedence difference lives in the code the declaration dispatches through, not in any name, module path, or anchor field; DESIGN section 4c makes annotations invisible to semantic passes, and a scope block's citation is an annotation about authority, not a type on the code path. So a fully green placement gate and a green namespace wave are both positive evidence about ownership and binding, and NO evidence about whether the decision the code implements is the one the standard defines.",

    "**THE SAME FAILURE WITHOUT A CITATION HUNK.** The false attestation propagates: any caller that takes a module's compared/verified value as standard-ordered inherits the gap, and any new comparison path added under an already-cited module inherits the citation without touching prose. Neither edit produces a citation hunk the recognition rule below would see, which is why the rule names the code path, not only the anchor.",

    "**RUNG: MITIGATABLE.** External review caught the instance; nothing in the tree can. The ceiling is not structural impossibility: whether a code path implements a cited algorithm is decidable for transcribed algorithms by comparing against a transcription datum of the standard's rules, so the honest ceiling is MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE. **NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a cited-surface census** -- an instrument that takes each ExternalModelScope's citation plus subject, extracts the normative operation the cited upstream defines for the surface the subject names (precedence, canonical form, grammar production), and surfaces, for adjudication, the actual code path the declaration dispatches through -- refusing the pairing until an adjudicator dispositions whether the path is that operation or a weaker one. That is a real instrument and it does not exist; naming it here is the trigger, not a claim of coverage.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: two triggers, and the second matters because the defect recurs with no citation hunk.** (1) Any diff that adds or re-points a citation onto a module whose declaration dispatches through a comparison, verification, or production path not named by the cited upstream's normative operation. (2) Any diff that adds or changes the comparison, verification, or production dispatch under a declaration whose module ALREADY carries a citation -- the citation hunk never appears in such a diff, so anchor-level review sees nothing, while the false attestation extends to the new path. A third channel needs no diff at all: upstream drift, where a cited page is revised under a static citation and the pairing silently becomes false. No repository specimen of that third channel has been confirmed yet (the wcag_contrast receipt looked like one and was not: the pairing was false when authored), so it is named here as a channel to watch, not a counted instance. The obligation is that a scope block which cites a standard has adopted the standard's ALGORITHM as an attestation: read the code path UNDER THE DISPATCHED PRODUCTION CALL, not only the anchor and not a helper beside the path, and either match it against the standard's normative operation or scope a weaker subject whose surface the path really implements. Reading the anchor alone is the half that manufactures the false attestation; reading a helper beside the production path is the other half, and both were done once each while authoring this row.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
